  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "If the speed of a rabbit is measured in $\\text{in/min},$ what are the units of its acceleration?",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{in/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{min}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/ecdd2f132499a6be0c1d8fa48f021713e2019ed9e6660af041853f7e30766091.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{in/min}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{in}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}.$",
